onkey to put its hand inside. Then you must fill the jar part way with rock=
s, so it is too heavy for a monkey to carry. Then you must to scatter some =
treats, attractive to monkeys, near the jar, to attract one, and put some m=
ore inside the jar. A monkey will come along, reach into the narrow opening=
, and grab while the grabbing=E2=80=99s good. But now he won=E2=80=99t be a=
ble to extract his fist, now full of treats, from the jar. Not without uncl=
enching his hand. Not without relinquishing what he already has. And that=
=E2=80=99s just what he won=E2=80=99t do. The monkey-catcher can just walk =
over to the jar and pick up the monkey. The animal will not sacrifice the p=
art to preserve the whole. <br><br>Something valuable, given up, ensures fu=
